WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.800
SignalR may use WebSockets, Server-Sent
Events,

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:11.944
or long polling depending on client
capability, server configuration, proxies,

3
00:00:12.024 --> 00:00:14.276
and hosting environment.

4
00:00:14.356 --> 00:00:19.270
Those transports support the same
application feature,

5
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:25.800
but they can have different effects on
authentication behavior, logging,

6
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.282
origin review, and operational
troubleshooting.

7
00:00:30.362 --> 00:00:34.764
For browser clients, CORS deserves direct
review.

8
00:00:34.844 --> 00:00:42.215
Cross-origin SignalR connections should be
allowed only for trusted and controlled

9
00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:46.492
origins that are part of the application
design.

10
00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:53.022
A broad or casual CORS policy can turn a
deployment setting into a long-term

11
00:00:53.102 --> 00:00:54.945
governance problem.

12
00:00:55.025 --> 00:01:02.396
The allowed origins should match real
client deployments and should be reviewed when

13
00:01:02.476 --> 00:01:07.287
environments, domains, or front-end
hosting changes.

14
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:13.408
WebSocket origin behavior needs separate
attention where relevant.

15
00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:19.732
CORS policy is not the complete story for
WebSocket origin restriction,

16
00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:25.341
so teams should confirm how their hosting
stack, reverse proxy,

17
00:01:25.421 --> 00:01:30.232
and application middleware treat WebSocket
requests.

18
00:01:30.312 --> 00:01:34.305
The goal is not to memorize a transport
detail,

19
00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:39.913
but to make origin handling an explicit
production review item.

20
00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:42.553
Access tokens also need care.

21
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.499
In some browser transport cases,

22
00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:51.722
tokens may appear in query strings because
of browser API limitations.

23
00:01:51.802 --> 00:01:57.228
That means URL logging, reverse proxy
logs, application logs,

24
00:01:57.308 --> 00:02:01.403
load balancer logs, diagnostics,
monitoring,

25
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:08.035
and error reporting should be reviewed so
sensitive values are not stored or

26
00:02:08.115 --> 00:02:10.674
forwarded unintentionally.

27
00:02:10.754 --> 00:02:17.409
Connection identifiers and connection
tokens should be treated carefully.

28
00:02:17.489 --> 00:02:24.552
They are part of connection management and
should not be casually exposed in logs,

29
00:02:24.632 --> 00:02:30.775
URLs, screenshots, support tickets, or
client-visible diagnostics.

30
00:02:30.855 --> 00:02:36.793
HTTPS should be the production baseline so
connection negotiation,

31
00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:43.220
transport upgrade, credentials, and
messages are protected in transit.
